Composite and rig the **Aegis**, a colossal multi-gun walking fortress that strides
on six independent legs — each a three-segment thigh/shin/flat-foot chain — whose main
cannon aims forward, whose two side-mounted turrets each traverse to cover only their
own flank, and whose radar vane sweeps on its own, as an 88x80x104 model **meshed with
Marching Cubes** using only the `mc-anim` tool. You build it not by painting cells but
by **compositing a signed-distance field** — adding and subtracting primitives — and
you author its walk and weapon animations as F-curves.

## How compositing and rigging works

The only way to build a part and edit the rig is the `mc-anim` binary already on your
`PATH`. It is the sole channel: you build the model by calling it **one operation at a
time**, and the geometry it **emits** — one meshed `parts/<part>.mesh.json` per part —
together with the rig you define are the **authoritative output that is scored**, not
any file you write yourself. Anything produced another way (writing a mesh directly,
editing a preview by hand) is discarded and flagged; only the tool-emitted meshes and
the rig count.

Unlike painting discrete voxels, `mc-anim` builds each part's geometry as a
**continuous signed-distance field**: you **add** primitives (spheres, boxes,
ellipsoids, cylinders — each a center/extent and an opaque `#rrggbb` color) to lay
down material and **subtract** them to carve it away, optionally with a soft `--blend`
radius that fuses primitives smoothly (a `--blend` of `0`, the default, is a hard
union with a genuine crease). The binary then extracts the field's surface into a
triangle mesh with **Marching Cubes** — a **fixed LOW-POLY, chunky faceted** character
(a coarse sample grid): it is what this binary is for, not a setting you tune. Lean
into the bold, faceted look.

Every operation targets a **part** with `--part <name>`; each part is a **separate
field** composited in the shared volume's coordinates. The field starts **empty**.
Colors are **opaque** `#rrggbb` — there is no transparency. After each call the binary
re-renders that part's wgpu preview to `parts/<part>.png` and updates that part's
emitted `parts/<part>.mesh.json`. Blank starting previews and a pre-seeded `rig.json`
holding the required parts, joints, and animation declarations are already in place at
`{{workspace}}`.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its part, for example:

```
mc-anim add-box --part chassis --cx 44 --cy 45 --cz 52 --sx 60 --sy 30 --sz 88 --color "#c69a4b"
mc-anim add-sphere --part main_turret --cx 44 --cy 64 --cz 56 --r 12 --blend 3 --color "#c69a4b"
mc-anim subtract-cylinder --part chassis --cx 44 --cy 60 --cz 96 --axis z --r 6 --length 20
```

Run `mc-anim --help` to list every field operation **and** the rig subcommands
(defining parts, setting pivots, defining joints, and defining animations and their
keyframes), and `mc-anim <operation> --help` for one operation's exact flags — that
help text is the authoritative contract. **Read `parts/<part>.png` after your calls**
to see that part's progress, **and read the assembled-scene previews under `scene/`
(`scene/iso.png`, `scene/front.png`, `scene/side.png`, `scene/top.png`)** — the whole
fortress composed from all your parts, re-rendered after every operation. The scene
views are how you confirm the parts actually fit together: that the six legs seat
under the hull and reach the ground, the main turret sits centered on the citadel, the
cannon meets the turret front, and each side turret seats out on its sponson. Because
each part is composited in the shared volume's coordinates, position each part where it
belongs on the finished fortress; the scene simply composes them there.

